## Why this topic matters

The Computer Misuse Act 1990 is the tier of CA 4.1 most often confused
with data protection law, and the confusion is what costs students Paper
1 marks. Many scenarios that look like data protection breaches at first
glance are actually computer misuse offences, and the distinction comes
down to which party is doing what.

The shortcut is to look at who is acting and what they are doing. If an
organisation is failing to handle personal data properly, the lead Act
is usually data protection. If an individual is accessing a system they
should not be accessing, the lead Act is usually computer misuse.
Scenarios often engage both, but the Name mark goes to whichever offence
matches the act being described.

## What the law actually is

The Computer Misuse Act 1990 was enacted after the 1988 House of Lords
decision in R v Gold and Schifreen, where two journalists had used
guessed credentials to access a BT Prestel service and view the Duke of
Edinburgh's mailbox. The prosecution tried to fit the conduct under the
Forgery and Counterfeiting Act 1981. The Lords held that no forgery had
been committed because the false credentials existed only momentarily
inside the machine. Parliament accepted the gap in English law and
passed the CMA 1990 to close it.

The Act has been amended twice in ways students should recognise:

- The Police and Justice Act 2006 raised the section 1 maximum sentence
  from six months to two years, added section 3A (making and supplying
  articles for use in offences), and broadened section 3.
- The Serious Crime Act 2015 added section 3ZA to deal with acts that
  cause or risk serious damage to human welfare, the environment, the
  economy or national security.

The current Act covers five distinct offences, set out in sections 1, 2,
3, 3ZA and 3A.

## The five offences

### Section 1: unauthorised access to computer material

The basic offence. A person commits an offence if they cause a computer
to perform any function with intent to secure access to programs or data
held in any computer, where the access is unauthorised and the person
knows it is unauthorised at the time. No further damage, copying or
sharing is required for the offence to be complete. Maximum sentence is
two years' imprisonment.

### Section 2: unauthorised access with intent

A more serious version of section 1. The person commits a section 1
offence and at the time of that access intends to commit (or to
facilitate the commission of) a further offence punishable by
imprisonment, such as fraud or blackmail. Maximum sentence is five
years' imprisonment.

### Section 3: unauthorised acts with intent to impair, or recklessness

The offence of doing an unauthorised act in relation to a computer where
the person intends to impair the operation of any computer, prevent or
hinder access to programs or data, impair the operation of any program,
or where the person is reckless as to whether any of those consequences
will occur. This is the section that covers releasing malware,
distributed denial-of-service attacks, deliberate data deletion and
ransomware deployment. Maximum sentence is ten years' imprisonment.

### Section 3ZA: unauthorised acts causing serious damage

Added in 2015 to deal with the most serious computer attacks. The
offence applies where an unauthorised section 3 act causes or creates a
significant risk of serious damage of a material kind: damage to human
welfare in a country (including loss of life, injury, illness or
disruption of essential supplies), damage to the environment, damage to
the economy, or damage to national security. Maximum sentence is life
imprisonment where the damage relates to human welfare or national
security, fourteen years otherwise.

### Section 3A: making, supplying or obtaining articles

A wrapper offence for the tools used in CMA offences. Making, adapting,
supplying or offering to supply an article (typically a piece of malware
or a hacking tool) for use in a section 1, 2 or 3 offence
is itself an offence. So is obtaining the article with a view to its
use. Maximum sentence is two years' imprisonment.

## What "unauthorised" actually means

The single most common misunderstanding in CMA scenarios is treating
authorisation as a property of the person rather than of the task. A
person who has been given a username and password is authorised to do
some things with that login. They are not necessarily authorised to do
everything that the login technically allows.

The Act treats access as unauthorised if the person is not entitled to
control access of the kind in question, and does not have consent from
someone who is entitled to give that consent. A bank employee who is
authorised to view customer accounts for processing legitimate enquiries
is not authorised to view the account of a celebrity client out of
curiosity. A police officer who is authorised to query the Police
National Computer for operational reasons is not authorised to query a
neighbour's record because they are nosy. Both have lawful credentials.
Both commit the section 1 offence the moment they exceed the
task-specific scope and know they are exceeding it.

This task-specific reading is the part of the Act that most often
catches insiders rather than outside attackers, and is the part Paper 1
scenarios most often test.

## What "computer" actually means

The Act does not define the word "computer". This is deliberate. The
1990 drafters anticipated that defining the term would date the
legislation as technology evolved, and the courts have since applied
the Act to anything that processes data digitally. Smartphones, smart
cards, payment terminals, networked appliances and embedded devices
have all been treated as computers under the Act.

For Paper 1 purposes, treat any device that processes digital
information as a computer for CMA purposes, regardless of what the
scenario calls it.

## Enforcement and consequences

CMA offences are prosecuted by the Crown Prosecution Service (or by
specialist units such as the National Crime Agency's National Cyber
Crime Unit for serious cases). Sentences vary by section as set out
above. The Act has extraterritorial reach: an offence can be prosecuted
in the UK where there is a significant link to the UK, whether that
link is established through the location of the perpetrator, the
location of the targeted computer, the location of the affected
parties, or the nationality of any of them.

Beyond criminal liability, a CMA conviction has practical consequences
that scenarios often ask students to identify: dismissal from current
employment, lasting damage to professional references, exclusion from
safeguarding-relevant or regulated roles, and difficulty obtaining
clearance for future technical work.

## What Paper 1 expects

When a Paper 1 scenario shows someone accessing or interfering with a
computer system, the path through the answer is:

1. **Name the Act and the section.** "The Computer Misuse Act 1990,
   section [1 / 2 / 3 / 3ZA / 3A]." Where the question rewards a more
   specific Name, also state which kind of offence the section
   describes.

2. **Explain what the section actually requires.** State the elements
   of the offence (unauthorised access, knowledge of unauthorisation,
   plus any intent or recklessness requirement) in your own words.

3. **Apply the elements to the scenario.** Tie each element to a fact
   in the scenario. The strongest answers quote the scenario explicitly:
   "because the worker was only authorised to reset passwords, not to
   read the headteacher's email..."

Recognising CMA versus data protection at the boundary is the recurring
trap. When the question is about an individual exceeding their access,
the Act is almost always the CMA. When the question is about an
organisation handling personal data badly, the Act is almost always the
DPA. Many real scenarios involve both, and the strongest answers name
both, but the primary Name follows the act being described.

The worked examples in the Method section show this path running on
past-paper questions. Once you have practised the assessment below,
come back to those.
